{"causes": ["The death of Manuel I of Trebizond in 1263 led to Andronikos II's unexpected ascension to the imperial throne at a young age.", "The ongoing territorial ambitions of the Seljuk Turks, particularly under the powerful Pervane, created constant pressure on Trebizond's borders.", "Trebizond's strategic location on the Black Sea coast made its ports, especially Sinope, highly coveted by neighboring powers for trade and naval control."], "effects": ["The definitive loss of Sinope in 1265 severely crippled Trebizond's maritime trade and strategic influence in the Black Sea.", "Andronikos II's premature death in 1266 plunged Trebizond into further dynastic instability, leading to the brief reign of George I.", "The weakening of Trebizond's position encouraged further Seljuk and later Ottoman encroachments on its Anatolian territories."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "manuel-i-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Manuel I of Trebizond", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "targetName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "context": "Andronikos II succeeded his father, Manuel I, as Emperor of Trebizond in 1263."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "fall-of-sinope-1265", "targetName": "Fall of Sinope (1265)", "context": "Andronikos II's reign saw the critical loss of Sinope to the Seljuk Turks in 1265."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "trebizond", "targetName": "Trebizond", "context": "Andronikos II reigned as Emperor of Trebizond from 1263 to 1266, with Trebizond serving as the capital of his empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane", "sourceName": "Mu'in al-Din Suleyman Pervane", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "targetName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "context": "Mu'in al-Din Suleyman Pervane's military actions, specifically the capture of Sinope in 1265, profoundly impacted Andronikos II's reign and the Empire of Trebizond."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "george-i-of-trebizond", "targetName": "George I of Trebizond", "context": "Andronikos II's premature death in 1266 directly led to his younger brother George I's ascension to the Trapezuntine throne."}], "places": [{"name": "Trebizond, Byzantine Empire", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Sinope, Byzantine Empire", "role": "Major Port Loss"}, {"name": "Anatolia, Byzantine Empire", "role": "Region of Rule"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Andronikos II of Trebizond", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q452010", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_II_of_Trebizond"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Andronikos II's short reign, marked by the definitive loss of Sinope, fundamentally altered Trebizond's economic and strategic position, initiating a period of decline after his father's strong rule. This event had lasting consequences for Black Sea trade and regional power dynamics for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:15:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Andronikos II Megas Komnenos, or Andronicus II Grand Comnenus, was the Emperor of Trebizond from 1263 to 1266. Despite being the designated successor of his father Manuel I, Andronikos' tenure was short due to premature death from unrecorded causes. The major event of his reign was the definitive loss of Sinope to the Seljuk Turks under the regency of Mu'in al-Din Suleyman, also known as the Pervane, in the summer or fall of 1265. The capture of Sinope by the Turks gave them the best port on the Black Sea, allowing them to create a navy and compete with the Trapezuntines for influence in the Black Sea.", "newValue": "Andronikos II Megas Komnenos, Emperor of Trebizond from 1263 to 1266, presided over a pivotal and ultimately calamitous period for the Byzantine successor state in northeastern Anatolia. Ascending to the throne as a youth following the death of his powerful father, Manuel I, Andronikos inherited an empire at its zenith but rapidly facing aggressive external pressures. His brief reign is primarily remembered for the definitive loss of the crucial port city of Sinope to the Seljuk Turks, an event that reshaped the geopolitical landscape of the Black Sea and permanently diminished Trebizond's commercial and strategic standing.

Despite being the designated heir, Andronikos's youth meant his rule was largely overseen by a regency, likely influenced by his mother, Theodora. The most significant event of his short tenure was the fall of Sinope in the summer or autumn of 1265. This strategic city, which had been a Trapezuntine possession since 1214, was captured by the forces of the powerful Seljuk vizier Mu'in al-Din Suleyman, known as the Pervane. The loss of Sinope not only deprived Trebizond of its most vital Black Sea harbor and a significant source of customs revenue but also marked a profound shift in regional power dynamics, cementing Seljuk control over the southern Black Sea coast.

Andronikos II's premature death from unrecorded causes in 1266, at an age estimated to be around sixteen, plunged Trebizond into renewed dynastic uncertainty. The loss of Sinope, coupled with his untimely demise, initiated a period of increased vulnerability for the empire, foreshadowing subsequent territorial losses and internal strife. This era contrasted sharply with the stability and prosperity enjoyed under his father, setting a precarious precedent for his successors, particularly his brother George I and later John II, who would contend with an increasingly fragmented and hostile Anatolian environment."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:15:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1240, Trabzon"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1240, Trabzon"}], "newValue": ["The death of Manuel I of Trebizond in 1263 led to Andronikos II's unexpected ascension to the imperial throne at a young age.", "The ongoing territorial ambitions of the Seljuk Turks, particularly under the powerful Pervane, created constant pressure on Trebizond's borders.", "Trebizond's strategic location on the Black Sea coast made its ports, especially Sinope, highly coveted by neighboring powers for trade and naval control."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:15:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1266"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1266"}], "newValue": ["The definitive loss of Sinope in 1265 severely crippled Trebizond's maritime trade and strategic influence in the Black Sea.", "Andronikos II's premature death in 1266 plunged Trebizond into further dynastic instability, leading to the brief reign of George I.", "The weakening of Trebizond's position encouraged further Seljuk and later Ottoman encroachments on its Anatolian territories."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:15:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "RULES", "targetSlug": "field-221", "targetName": "Monarchs & Rulers", "context": "Andronikos II of Trebizond: Sovereign authority in monarchs & rulers"}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval", "targetName": "Medieval Era", "context": "Andronikos II of Trebizond was active during the Medieval era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "manuel-i-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Manuel I of Trebizond", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "targetName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "context": "Andronikos II succeeded his father, Manuel I, as Emperor of Trebizond in 1263."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "fall-of-sinope-1265", "targetName": "Fall of Sinope (1265)", "context": "Andronikos II's reign saw the critical loss of Sinope to the Seljuk Turks in 1265."}, {"sourceSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "sourceName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "trebizond", "targetName": "Trebizond", "context": "Andronikos II reigned as Emperor of Trebizond from 1263 to 1266, with Trebizond serving as the capital of his empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane", "sourceName": "Mu'in al-Din Suleyman Pervane", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "andronikos-ii-of-trebizond", "targetName": "Andronikos II of Trebizond", "context"